{"tag":"7UE3","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"theorem-1","file":"","label":"thm:main","number":"1.1","section":"sec-1","order":1,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:main}\nThere is no Tribonacci number that is a palindromic concatenation of\ntwo distinct repdigits.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:main"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":169,"to":172},"hash":"sha256:99520f7ab5d257fd38cfab077faf595001b1749c2655d9b60e6aa0ccf943f068","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"PY97","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-1","file":"","label":"lem:height","number":"2.1","section":"sec-3","order":2,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:height}\nThe algebraic number $g(\\alpha)=\\alpha/\\Psi'(\\alpha)$ has minimal\npolynomial $44x^3-2x-1$ over $\\mathbb Q$ and absolute logarithmic Weil\nheight\n\\[\nh(g(\\alpha))=\\frac{\\log44}{3}=1.26139654\\ldots.\n\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:height"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":242,"to":249},"hash":"sha256:47f9548f4d2fd8fd1ddc8e59cece032101aa95b0b19531eab1df554a2686c62e","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"I95J","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":3,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Working in the basis $\\{1,\\alpha,\\alpha^2\\}$ of $K:=\\mathbb Q(\\alpha)$\n(where $\\alpha^3=\\alpha^2+\\alpha+1$), the extended Euclidean algorithm\napplied, in exact rational arithmetic, to $\\Psi'(x)=3x^2-2x-1$ and $\\Psi(x)$\ngives\n\\[\ng(\\alpha)=\\frac{-4-3\\alpha+5\\alpha^2}{22}.\n\\]\nThe matrix of multiplication by $g(\\alpha)$ on $K$ in this basis has\ntrace $0$, sum of principal $2\\times2$ minors $-1/22$, and determinant\n$1/44$; its characteristic polynomial is therefore\n$x^3-\\tfrac1{22}x-\\tfrac1{44}$, which clears to the primitive integer\npolynomial $44x^3-2x-1$. By the rational root test (divisors of $1$ and\nof $44$), this polynomial has no rational root, so it is indeed the\nminimal polynomial of $g(\\alpha)$ over $\\mathbb Q$, of degree $3$. Its\nthree roots are $g(\\alpha)\\approx0.336228$ and a complex conjugate pair\nof modulus $\\approx0.260$, all of modulus $\u003c1$; hence $\\log^+|\\cdot|=0$\nat each root, and\n\\[\nh(g(\\alpha))=\\frac13\\Big(\\log44+\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\log^+|g(\\alpha)_i|\\Big)=\\frac{\\log44}{3}.\\qedhere\n\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":251,"to":272},"hash":"sha256:ca8348356001fc99f34c5b905a2a47a036df567f9bf5f1fd9f335ab2887ed49e","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"95VL","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"theorem-2","file":"","label":"Matveev11","number":"2.2","section":"sec-4","order":4,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{Matveev11} Let $\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_t$ be positive real algebraic numbers in a real algebraic number field \n$\\mathbb{K}$ of degree $D$, $b_1,\\ldots,b_t$ be nonzero integers, and assume that\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{eq:Lambda}\n\\Lambda:=\\gamma_1^{b_1}\\cdots\\gamma_t^{b_t} - 1,\n\\end{equation}\nis nonzero. Then\n$$\n\\log |\\Lambda| \u003e -1.4\\times 30^{t+3}\\times t^{4.5}\\times D^{2}(1+\\log D)(1+\\log B)A_1\\cdots A_t,\n$$\nwhere\n$$\nB\\geq\\max\\{|b_1|, \\ldots, |b_t|\\},\n$$\nand\n$$A\n_i \\geq \\max\\{Dh(\\gamma_i), |\\log\\gamma_i|, 0.16\\},\\qquad {\\text{for all}}\\qquad i=1,\\ldots,t.\n$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","name":"Matveev","labels":["Matveev11","eq:Lambda"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":291,"to":309},"hash":"sha256:6f658722e49361906cf3daa96278b55b04acceb55374158ccbfe6959d6832795","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"V5IR","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-2","file":"","label":"Dujjella","number":"2.3","section":"sec-5","order":5,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{Dujjella}\nLet $M$ be a positive integer, $p/q$ be a convergent of the continued fraction of the irrational number $\\tau$ such that $q\u003e6M$, and  $A,B,\\mu$ be some real numbers with $A\u003e0$ and $B\u003e1$. Let further \n$\\varepsilon: = ||\\mu q||-M||\\tau q||$. If $ \\varepsilon \u003e 0 $, then there is no solution to the inequality\n$$\n0\u003c|u\\tau-v+\\mu|\u003cAB^{-w},\n$$\nin positive integers $u,v$ and $w$ with\n$$ \nu\\le M \\quad {\\text{and}}\\quad w\\ge \\dfrac{\\log(Aq/\\varepsilon)}{\\log B}.\n$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","name":"Dujella, Peth\\H o","labels":["Dujjella"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":316,"to":326},"hash":"sha256:e58600a136dee522a518963f7d49950cdb12e41b1b0ad342e354ec31ee17d864","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"WTDK","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-3","file":"","label":"eqn1","number":"2.4","section":"sec-5","order":6,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"{\\label{eqn1}}\nIf $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ satisfies $\\left|x\\right|\u003c\\frac{1}{2}$, then $\\left|\\log(1+x)\\right|\u003c\\frac{3}{2}\\left|x\\right|.$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["eqn1"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":345,"to":348},"hash":"sha256:d7f5e3a884db3247b09444fe8aa6f3fffbd17c70d826cc5e177d9192d9cde18a","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"SMXG","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-4","file":"","label":"lem:size","number":"3.1","section":"sec-7","order":7,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:size}\nEvery solution of \\eqref{eq:main} with $\\ell,m\\ge1$ satisfies\n\\[\n|n-3.778583\\,N|\u003c2\n\\quad\\hbox{with}\\quad N=2\\ell+m,\n\\]\nwhere $\\frac{\\log 10}{\\log \\alpha}\u003c3.778583.$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:size"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":381,"to":388},"hash":"sha256:34814c0db218fc955ea808a943b096f15bc892a2dc9d48e96e22b79edc1dc2fe","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"SH2K","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"proof-2","file":"","section":"sec-7","order":8,"title":"Proof","body_md":"By \\eqref{eq:shape}, $T_n$ has exactly $N=2\\ell+m$ decimal digits, so\n$10^{N-1}\\le T_n\u003c10^N$, and in particular $10^{N-1}\\le9T_n+d_1\u003c10^{N+1}$. On the other hand, by\n\\eqref{eq:binet}, $T_n=g(\\alpha)\\alpha^n+e(n)$, where\n$|e(n)|\\le2|g(\\beta)||\\beta|^n\u003c2\\cdot0.26\\cdot0.7374\u003c0.4$ for $n\\ge1$. Hence\n$$10^{N-1}-0.4\u003cg(\\alpha)\\alpha^n\u003c10^N+0.4.$$\nSince $N\\ge3$, we have $10^{N-1}-0.4\\ge0.996\\cdot10^{N-1}$ and $10^N+0.4\\le1.0004\\cdot10^N$.\nTaking logarithms, and using $n\\log\\alpha=\\log\\big(g(\\alpha)\\alpha^n\\big)-\\log g(\\alpha)$ with\n$-\\log g(\\alpha)\u003c1.0899$, we get\n$$-1.217\u003cn\\log\\alpha-N\\log10\u003c1.091.$$\nDividing by $\\log\\alpha=0.6093$ gives $|n-3.778583\\,N|\u003c1.217/0.6093\u003c2$, which is the claim.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-4","attach":3,"span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":390,"to":401},"hash":"sha256:16c01b4064972c96fbc26424f2612947c611ae03ff4762577a380fabd4706f79","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#proof-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"88WY","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-5","file":"","label":"thm:ell-bound","number":"3.2","section":"sec-7","order":9,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{thm:ell-bound}\nEvery solution $(n,\\ell,m,d_1,d_2)$ of \\eqref{eq:main} with $\\ell,m\\ge1$\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\ell\u003cF(n):=\\frac{\\log12+1.94\\times 10^{14}(1+\\log n)}{\\log10}.\n\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:ell-bound"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":467,"to":473},"hash":"sha256:b257b15b52b3277799bb945a655dad655505a73ffd2de8ba61e0ce60e072091f","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"W1N5","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-6","file":"","label":"lem:identity2","number":"3.3","section":"sec-7","order":10,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:identity2}\nFor all $\\ell,m\\ge1$ and $1\\le d_1\\le9$, $0\\le d_2\\le9$,\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:identity2}\n9T_n-(d_1-d_2)\\cdot10^{\\ell}+d_1=10^{\\ell+m}\\cdot M(\\ell),\n\\qquad M(\\ell):=d_1\\cdot10^{\\ell}+d_2-d_1.\n\\end{equation}\nMoreover $M(\\ell)=d_1(10^\\ell-1)+d_2\\ge10^\\ell-1\\ge10^\\ell/2$ for all\n$\\ell\\ge1$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:identity2","eq:identity2"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":494,"to":502},"hash":"sha256:0aee5da76971fec333c4f3e821875926c9156a5784256fb73c929c22782668e7","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"N6UE","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"proof-3","file":"","section":"sec-7","order":11,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Identity \\eqref{eq:identity2} follows from \\eqref{eq:main9} and\n$10^{N}=10^{\\ell}\\cdot10^{\\ell+m}$; it was verified\ndirectly, as an identity of integers, for all $1\\le\\ell\\le5$,\n$1\\le d_1\\le9$, $0\\le d_2\\le9$ ($d_1\\ne d_2$), $m\\in\\{1,3,7\\}$. The\nlower bound on $M(\\ell)$ follows from $d_1\\ge1$, $d_2\\ge0$, and\n$10^\\ell-1\\ge10^\\ell/2$ for $\\ell\\ge1$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-6","attach":3,"span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":504,"to":511},"hash":"sha256:af3efe3938bbe8eac60e01d85aa86469afa9171360e8cdb11ff108a228b4a34b","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#proof-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"EQ71","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-7","file":"","label":"thm:final-bound","number":"3.4","section":"sec-7","order":12,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{thm:final-bound}\nEvery solution $(n,\\ell,m,d_1,d_2)$ of \\eqref{eq:main} with $\\ell,m\\ge1$\nsatisfies\n\\[\nn\u003c1.3\\times10^{32}, \\quad \\ell\u003c6.4\\times10^{15} \\quad \\hbox{and}\\quad \\ell+m\u003c1.7\\times10^{31}\n\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:final-bound"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":605,"to":611},"hash":"sha256:bfee91f294dee94c46fe0072af8e219c4a55084dd2d8ae5bbc531936bfb7782e","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"7H0R","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-8","file":"","label":"prop:ell-reduced","number":"3.5","section":"sec-8","order":13,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{prop:ell-reduced}\nEvery solution of \\eqref{eq:main} with $\\ell,m\\ge1$ satisfies\n$\\ell\\le36$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["prop:ell-reduced"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":664,"to":667},"hash":"sha256:1c945fde07360e4f02550672e9a2a0faebcd9a720c0b1009e8b3c848c91bd289","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"SEVF","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15770","version":"1","local":"lemma-9","file":"","label":"prop:lm-reduced","number":"3.6","section":"sec-8","order":14,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{prop:lm-reduced}\nEvery solution of \\eqref{eq:main} with $\\ell,m\\ge1$ satisfies\n$\\ell+m\\le38$, and consequently $m\\le37$ and $N=2\\ell+m\\le74$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["prop:lm-reduced"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"tribonacci_palindrome_concatenation_v4.tex","from":689,"to":692},"hash":"sha256:5565fe56a5fbf832d78b5bc3ae475eff4a6d5fefc797ada74a8b9b9602384f69","minhash":"","census":"2610.15770v1#env-11","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
